# Readability control-flow rules

## Logical operators in conditions

- Do not use `||`, `&&`, `and`, `or`, or `xor` directly inside `if`,
  `elseif`, or `while` conditions.
- Extract condition logic to a descriptive boolean variable first, then use
  that variable in the control statement.

```php
// Bad.
if (!isset($account->realname) || !mb_strlen($account->realname)) {
}

// Good.
$realname_is_missing = !isset($account->realname) || !mb_strlen($account->realname);
if ($realname_is_missing) {
}
```

```php
// Bad.
if (isset($account->realname) && mb_strlen($account->realname)) {
}

// Good.
$has_realname = isset($account->realname) && mb_strlen($account->realname);
if ($has_realname) {
}
```

### Boolean variable naming

- Names must describe the meaning of the comparison:
  `$has_realname`, `$field_is_empty`, `$may_show_title`,
  `$should_skip_account`.
- Prefer positive names where practical.
- One meaningful variable per condition is preferred. Nested `if` blocks are
  acceptable when they improve readability.

### Exceptions

- `??`, `?:`, comparisons, `instanceof`, and `match` expressions are allowed.
- This rule applies only to control-structure conditions, not to expressions in
  the body.

## Increment and decrement operators

- Do not use `++`, `--`, `++$x`, or `$x++`.
- Use `+= 1` and `-= 1` instead.

```php
// Bad.
for ($i = 0; $i < strlen($format); $i++) {
}
$depth++;
$limit--;

// Good.
for ($i = 0; $i < strlen($format); $i += 1) {
}
$depth += 1;
$limit -= 1;
```

```php
// Bad.
['@number' => ++$footer_notes_counter];

// Good.
$footer_notes_counter += 1;
['@number' => $footer_notes_counter];
```

- String literals that contain `--` (for example `'-- --'`) are not increment
  or decrement usage.
- This project intentionally diverges from common Drupal style here for local
  readability.
